---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import NavBar from "../components/NavBar.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import Divider from "../components/Divider.astro";
---

<Layout title="ZIPTIP — NICHOLAS SOBCHAK">
  <NavBar />
  <main class="mx-auto w-full max-w-4xl px-6 pb-16 pt-16 md:px-10 md:pb-32 md:pt-24">
    <header class="mb-12 flex flex-col items-center gap-6 text-center">
      <img
        src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/ziptip-logo.png`}
        alt="ZipTip logo"
        width={499}
        height={504}
        class="h-48 w-48 object-contain md:h-64 md:w-64"
      />
      <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.3em] text-gold">
        Cashless Tipping for Hospitality Workers
      </p>
      <a
        href="https://ziptip.io"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        class="border border-gold/60 px-5 py-3 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.25em] text-gold transition-colors duration-200 hover:bg-gold/10"
      >
        [ Visit ZipTip ]
      </a>
    </header>

    <Divider />

    <section class="mt-10">
      <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.3em] text-gold">
        [ What Is ZipTip? ]
      </p>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl font-academic text-lg leading-relaxed text-parchment md:text-xl">
        ZipTip is the world’s first and only cashless tipping platform built purposely for the hospitality ecosystem.
        With secure, scannable touchpoints tailored for every position, 
        rewarding great service has never been easier.<br />
        <br />
        <strong>It starts with a tap.</strong><br />
        Tippers simply scan or tap their phone to a ZipTip Touchpoint. 
        Instantly, like magic, the Service Professional’s profile appears on the screen—no clunky app downloads required.<br />
        <br />
        <strong>Then come the Tip.</strong><br />
        The guest selects from three smart, pre-set tip amounts or enters a custom gratuity of their own.<br />
        <br />
        <strong>Done.</strong><br />
        That’s it. Really. By that evening, the funds are securely deposited directly 
        into the Service Pro’s account or seamlessly routed to the Operator’s ERP for payroll. 
        Appreciation is expressed and accepted. Karma is activated. All without cash.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12">
      <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.3em] text-gold">
        [ The Mission ]
      </p>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl font-academic text-lg leading-relaxed text-parchment md:text-xl">
        Hospitality workers rely on tips as a significant portion of their income,
        yet cash tipping has been declining for years. 
        Hospitality professionals miss out on $750,000,000 in tips each year because half of us don't carry cash. 
        ZipTip exists to close that gap — making tipping as easy as tapping a phone, 
        so workers get fairly compensated for the service they provide.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12">
      <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.3em] text-gold">
        [ My Role ]
      </p>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl font-academic text-lg leading-relaxed text-parchment md:text-xl">
        I joined ZipTip as a <strong>Founding Software Developer in December 2025</strong> and have worked
        across the full stack — the AWS Lambda backend, the Angular provider app, and the tipper-facing
        web properties. Between December 2025 and August 2026 I landed <strong>40 commits</strong> across the
        platform's repos: <strong>provider-api</strong> (Python / AWS SAM), <strong>serviceProviderApp</strong>
        (Angular + Capacitor), and <strong>ZipTipTipperPage</strong>. Here's what I built.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-10 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.3em] text-gold">
        [ The Scan API — The Headline Piece ]
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl font-academic text-lg leading-relaxed text-parchment md:text-xl">
        The product's core magic is the QR touchpoint: a guest scans a code and the professional's tipping
        profile appears instantly. That redirection pipeline was greenfield, and I built it end to end.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl font-academic text-lg leading-relaxed text-parchment md:text-xl">
        A scanable id like <span class="text-bone">https://scan.ziptip.io/scanable[ID]</span> must resolve
        to the service professional's tip page — <span class="text-bone">tip.ziptip.io/id/[accountID]</span> —
        as an HTTP redirect, fast. I created the <strong>ZipTipScannables</strong> DynamoDB table to hold the
        touchpoint registry, then wrote a Lambda with scoped permissions to read from <em>both</em> the
        scannables table and the user data table, perform the id → account mapping, and answer with an HTTP
        redirect. I wired it behind a custom API Gateway endpoint (<strong>ScanApiEndpoint</strong>), configured
        the <span class="text-bone">scan.ziptip.io</span> custom domain, and mapped the domain to the stage —
        including the DNS/API-Gateway round-trip where the dev and prod mappings disagreed, which I resolved by
        learning which stage was authoritative. I also added <strong>auth to the redirect flow</strong> so the
        <span class="text-bone">Authorization</span> header is honored before the redirect, keeping the surface
        from becoming an open proxy — and I shipped unit coverage for the router in a
        <span class="text-bone">tests/touchpoint/router/unit/test_router.py</span> suite.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-10 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.3em] text-gold">
        [ Authentication & Account Signup ]
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl font-academic text-lg leading-relaxed text-parchment md:text-xl">
        The original signup flow used <strong>Username = phone</strong>, which the Cognito user-pool client
        rejects with an <span class="text-bone">InvalidParameter</span> error as soon as phone formatting varies.
        I fixed it by switching the identity to <strong>email</strong>, aligning the handler with what the user
        pool actually accepts; writing phone validation (normalization + rules) as a <strong>reusable
        validator</strong> rather than inline string checks; adding <strong>JSON request tests</strong>
        (<span class="text-bone">AuthSignUpFunction</span>) so the handler's contract is pinned down; and
        handling duplicate IDs so a re-submitted signup fails cleanly instead of corrupting state. It taught me
        the value of reading the provider docs before writing code — the "invalid parameter" failure was upstream
        behavior I'd assumed, not a bug in my handler.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-10 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.3em] text-gold">
        [ Open Graph & Link Previews ]
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl font-academic text-lg leading-relaxed text-parchment md:text-xl">
        Every tip link shares a message on social, so I added <strong>Open Graph tags for rich link
        previews</strong> with generated message graphics, then refined the placard-drawing logic and cleaned up
        the response builders that serve them. The result: a tipped message pastes with a real preview card
        instead of a bare URL — a small touch that lifts how the product is shared every day.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-10 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.3em] text-gold">
        [ CI/CD Pipeline ]
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl font-academic text-lg leading-relaxed text-parchment md:text-xl">
        I built and iterated on the <strong>AWS SAM pipeline configuration</strong> for dev and prod, fixing the
        prod dependency ordering that broke deploy chains, and <strong>enabled rollback support</strong> so a bad
        deploy can be undone rather than hotfixed blind. I debugged the <strong>boto3 credential error</strong> in
        CI and added the test configuration + event/script scaffolding that made the suite run green. Coming back
        after a stint away, I removed stale folders (aws-sam, auth roots) and resolved branch-stash and
        merge-merge drift that had accumulated between the <span class="text-bone">nick</span>,
        <span class="text-bone">main</span>, and <span class="text-bone">dev</span> branches.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-10 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.3em] text-gold">
        [ The Provider App — Dev Tooling & A Town-Level Refactor ]
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl font-academic text-lg leading-relaxed text-parchment md:text-xl">
        <span class="text-bone">serviceProviderApp</span> is an Angular 20 + Capacitor app for the professionals
        themselves: signup, Stripe onboarding handoff, SMS/Cognito passwordless auth, profile creation, band QR
        linking, pool-tip claiming, and a dashboard with a tipping QR that can be added to Apple Wallet. On the
        frontend I owned a <strong>code-quality push</strong> across two passes.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl font-academic text-lg leading-relaxed text-parchment md:text-xl">
        First, a tooling baseline: I introduced <strong>ESLint, Prettier, and a Husky pre-commit hook</strong>
        across the app, added a <span class="text-bone">.browserslistrc</span> targeting mobile-first browsers
        (the app's primary surface is a phone), and consolidated scattered environment config under
        <span class="text-bone">src/environments</span>.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl font-academic text-lg leading-relaxed text-parchment md:text-xl">
        Then, the biggest single commit I've made at ZipTip: "Type API contracts and migrate services to
        <span class="text-bone">inject()</span> for lint compliance" — <strong>28 files, +660/−540</strong> across
        components, services, guards, and interfaces: <span class="text-bone">api.service.ts</span>,
        <span class="text-bone">profile.service.ts</span>, <span class="text-bone">SignUpCoordinator.service.ts</span>,
        the dashboard/profile/phone-verify/sms-login components, the auth guard, and new typed endpoint interfaces
        (<span class="text-bone">endpoint-interfaces/response.interface.ts</span>). Mechanically it was a migration
        from constructor DI to Angular's <span class="text-bone">inject()</span>; substantively it put a
        <strong>typed contract on every API response the app consumes</strong> instead of <span class="text-bone">any</span>.
        I also fixed lint errors in freshly merged features (band-linking, claim-tip, dashboard), replaced synthetic
        identifiers in the band-linking tests so they assert on real data shapes, corrected the Amplify Node runtime
        + dependency lock in <span class="text-bone">amplify.yml</span>, kept the README and FEATURES docs in line
        with what the app actually does — and ripped Husky back out when <span class="text-bone">pre-commit</span>
        proved more ceremony than value in this repo.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12">
      <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.3em] text-gold">
        [ What I've Learned ]
      </p>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl font-academic text-lg leading-relaxed text-parchment md:text-xl">
        The through-line across all four repos is a bias toward durable foundations:
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl font-academic text-lg leading-relaxed text-parchment md:text-xl">
        <strong>Redirects are a product feature, not plumbing.</strong><br />
        The scan flow had to be fast, authenticated, and testable — so it's a real Lambda with real tests and real
        domain routing.<br />
        <br />
        <strong>Read the provider's docs before writing the handler.</strong><br />
        The phone-identifier assumption cost a debugging session that the docs would have saved outright.<br />
        <br />
        <strong>Typed contracts beat any.</strong><br />
        A migration that touches 28 files pays off every time a service or endpoint changes shape afterward.<br />
        <br />
        <strong>Tooling is a product decision.</strong><br />
        ESLint/Prettier/pre-commit set the bar for everyone merging into the app, and removing Husky when it stopped
        earning its place was the same judgment in reverse.<br />
        <br />
        <strong>Pipelines earn rollback.</strong><br />
        CI/CD work that lets a deploy be undone is developer insurance that matures quietly.<br />
      </p>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl font-academic text-lg leading-relaxed text-parchment md:text-xl">
        It's been a unique experience building a product from the ground up — making foundational decisions that
        will scale with the business, and seeing direct impact on real workers who depend on the platform. The scan
        pipeline, the signup auth, the link previews, and the provider-app refactors are the pieces I can point to
        and say: that was mine.
      </p>
    </section>

  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>